# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT COSMETIC
#
# Committing the first PDF produced this warning:
#     warning: in the working copy of 'AGENTIC-ARBITER/demo/report.pdf',
#              LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it
#
# The blob that went in was byte-identical, so nothing looked wrong. But that warning is about the
# NEXT checkout: with core.autocrlf active on Windows, git would rewrite every 0x0A inside the PDF
# to 0x0D 0x0A on clone. A PDF carries a byte-offset xref table, so shifting bytes invalidates every
# offset in it -- the file opens to an error, or not at all.
#
# The failure mode is the worst kind for a submission: it works perfectly in this working tree and
# breaks for a judge who clones the repository. Same for the screening imagery, which is the
# evidence behind "five sites screened, two refused".
#
# `-text` means: never do end-of-line conversion on these. `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`.

*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.npy   binary
*.npz   binary
*.xlsx  binary

# Source and data stay text so diffs remain readable; git normalises them in the repository and
# checks them out with the platform's own endings, which is what it is for.
*.py    text
*.js    text
*.json  text
*.html  text
*.md    text
*.css   text
